  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning for the entire area through Sunday\nstill looks good. Outflow this morning has delayed the warming\nover the northern half of the Quad State, but it appears that\nmost of that area will still get to a heat index of 105 or \ngreater which is the warning threshold for a long duration (4+ \ndays) heat event. There is some potential for more dry outflow \nto reach the Evansville Tri State this evening, but that should \nnot have any impact on tomorrow's heat. It still looks like \nthere will be an upward trend in temperatures and a \ncorresponding downward trend in dewpoints through the weekend, \nbut the resulting heat index forecast will be virtually \nunchanged. Lows tonight and Saturday night are expected to be in\nthe middle to upper 70s, so there will be little relief from \nthe heat at night.\n\nThe ridge breaks down late Sunday, resulting in weak west\nnorthwest flow aloft over the Quad State Sunday night into\nMonday. At the surface a weak cold front will enter the area\nSunday night and push south of the area by the end of the day\nMonday. We will see if that happens, but the boundary will\nprovide a focus for convective potential Sunday night and\nMonday. Depending on the frontal timing, heat indices could\nclimb into the triple digits over the southern half of the\nregion Monday, which would support a Heat Advisory. There will\nbe no need to make that call until Sunday or even Monday morning\nwhen confidence in frontal location improves.\n\nAfter a dry day Tuesday with near normal temperatures, we will\nwarm back up on Wednesday when triple digit heat indices will\nreturn to the entire area. There are signs of a more substantial\nstorm system and cold front impacting the area Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. This would likely lead to a good chance of\nconvection followed by a more substantial cool down.",
